<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Louis Philippe I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1850">1850</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Louis-Philippe of Orléans, Louis Philippe, Duke of Orléans, Louis-Philippe</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Louis Philippe I was King of the French from 1830 to 1848. Head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> and son of a prince who had backed the Revolution, he was placed on the throne after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/july-revolution">July Revolution</a> of 1830 as a constitutional 'citizen king'. His July Monarchy favoured the wealthy middle class, and he was overthrown in the Revolution of 1848 and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i.jpg" alt="Louis Philippe I" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Xaver Winterhalter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1841_portrait_painting_of_Louis_Philippe_I_(King_of_the_French)_by_Winterhalter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 9 September 1842, under threat of bombardment, Pōmare IV and her leading chiefs signed a request for French protection, which <b>Louis-Philippe</b> ratified in March 1843.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XVIII granted France a Charter in 1814, and the July Monarchy of 1830 made <b>Louis Philippe I</b> “King of the French” rather than “King of France”.</blockquote><a href="/government/constitutional-monarchy">Constitutional monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Commissioned by Napoleon in 1806 to honour his armies, the Arc de Triomphe was finally inaugurated under King <b>Louis-Philippe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-29">29 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Three days of revolt in Paris drove him into exile, and <b>Louis-Philippe of Orléans</b> became king, beginning the July Monarchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/bourbon-restoration">Bourbon Restoration</a></li><li><blockquote>Liberal deputies offered the throne to his cousin <b>Louis-Philippe</b>, Duke of Orléans, who took the title King of the French and accepted a revised Charter that lowered property qualifications and limited royal power.</blockquote><a href="/state/july-monarchy">July Monarchy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Claremont, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1823" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Louis Philippe I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1823">What was here in 1823? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
